  { q: "A Nigerian production company owns a strong local brand and has experienced writers, directors, and producers, but lacks reliable cinema relationships and digital audience analytics. Using the Resource-Based View, which diagnosis is most defensible?", o: ["Its cinema relationships are external factors and therefore irrelevant to the firm’s internal competitive position", "Its lack of audience data proves that knowledge-based resources are less strategically important than physical equipment", "Its brand and talent guarantee sustained advantage because intangible resources cannot be weakened by distribution deficiencies", "Its valuable knowledge resources may create potential advantage, but missing complementary capabilities limit their effective exploitation"], a: 3, h: "Distinguish audience size from meaningful engagement, reliable data, monetisation, and the platform’s bargaining power." },
  { q: "A regulator is designing rules for cinemas, broadcasters, telecommunications providers, and OTT platforms operating in Nigeria’s converged media environment. Which policy problem requires the most sophisticated analysis?", o: ["Reconcile economic viability, competition, investment incentives, public-interest obligations, and differing speech or carriage principles", "Apply identical obligations to all providers because technological convergence eliminates meaningful institutional differences", "Exempt OTT services from regulation because internet distribution is inherently beyond national policy authority", "Prioritise consumer prices alone because affordability automatically resolves concerns about diversity and public accountability"], a: 0, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A producer is offered a co-production agreement in which the foreign partner supplies most financing, the Nigerian partner supplies local intellectual property and production access, and both claim final creative authority. Which contractual issue is most critical to resolve before production?", o: ["Transfer local intellectual property permanently in exchange for financing because international distribution requires complete ownership consolidation", "Divide creative control equally without specifying tie-breaking mechanisms because mutual trust prevents governance disputes", "Define contribution valuation, approval thresholds, chain of title, territorial rights, recoupment, profit sharing, and deadlock procedures", "Grant final authority to the largest financial contributor because cash investment determines every other form of project value"], a: 2, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A Nigerian film achieves strong festival recognition and critical praise but fails financially after an expensive theatrical campaign. A post-project review finds weak audience segmentation, poor release timing, and no negotiated secondary windows. Which conclusion demonstrates the deepest understanding of media value?", o: ["Critical recognition has little relevance to film value because only box-office revenue constitutes a valid outcome", "The production was necessarily artistically unsuccessful because financial loss indicates that audiences rejected its cultural meaning", "The failure proves that theatrical release is unsuitable for Nigerian films regardless of genre, audience, or market strategy", "Artistic and cultural value did not translate into commercial value because positioning, windowing, and distribution strategy were inadequate"], a: 3, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "An independent Nigerian producer is evaluating whether to retain an in-house post-production department or outsource editing, sound, and visual effects to specialist firms. The internal option offers tighter coordination but higher fixed costs; outsourcing offers flexibility but creates risks of opportunism, quality inconsistency, and knowledge leakage. Which analysis most appropriately integrates transaction-cost economics and media project management?", o: ["Compare coordination costs, asset specificity, monitoring, contractual safeguards, quality control, flexibility, and knowledge retention", "Prioritize the arrangement that maximizes immediate creative autonomy and minimizes the impact of external supplier-driven production delays", "Integrate all post-production permanently because vertical integration eliminates uncertainty", "Outsource whenever the quoted supplier price is below the internal wage bill"], a: 0, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A Nigerian streaming platform is considering an aggressive introductory subscription price to build its user base. Its value depends partly on attracting both audiences and advertisers, while exclusive content creates high sunk costs and network effects. Which economic interpretation is most rigorous?", o: ["Evaluate penetration pricing, cross-side network effects, switching costs, content investment, price elasticity, and the path to sustainable monetisation", "Offer content free permanently because larger audiences automatically guarantee profitable advertising", "Implement marginal cost pricing strategies, assuming that digital distribution entails negligible reproduction costs and minimal variable expenses per new user", "Avoid low introductory prices because price reductions necessarily destroy long-term brand equity"], a: 0, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A film enterprise’s board approves the acquisition of a Nigerian digital-content company after relying almost entirely on the chief executive’s convergence narrative. The acquisition later produces cultural conflict, debt pressure, duplicated operations, and audience decline. Which governance and strategic diagnosis is strongest?", o: ["Evidence that media convergence inevitably destroys value regardless of governance quality", "A normal post-merger adjustment, since cultural integration has little effect on media acquisition performance", "Weak independent oversight, inadequate due diligence, managerial overconfidence, poor integration planning, and failure to test strategic assumptions", "Transformational leadership that was ultimately undermined by temporary audience volatility and minor operational friction during the initial transition phase."], a: 2, h: "Distinguish audience size from meaningful engagement, reliable data, monetisation, and the platform’s bargaining power." },
  { q: "A government proposes a film-production incentive requiring local employment, cultural representation, and domestic exhibition. Producers argue that compliance costs may discourage private investment, while policymakers claim the scheme advances national cultural objectives. Which framework best evaluates the policy?", o: ["Approve the scheme on the basis that national cultural objectives and public interest mandates must always take precedence over private commercial constraints and market viability", "Weigh cultural and public-interest benefits against financial viability, investor incentives, administrative costs, political legitimacy, and policy sustainability", "Measure success only by the number of films produced, without examining quality, investment, access, or cultural outcomes", "Reject the scheme because any production requirement is incompatible with private enterprise"], a: 1, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A Nigerian production company has a distinctive storytelling culture, trusted local relationships, and skilled creative personnel, but its founder makes all decisions informally and the company struggles to scale across projects. Which management interpretation best explains the problem?", o: ["The company’s difficulty is primarily a copyright problem because organisational culture does not affect competitive performance", "Informal management is inherently superior for creative enterprises because formal structures suppress innovation and stifle the creative process.", "Scaling requires replacing local relationships with standardised international procedures", "Valuable knowledge-based resources are constrained by weak organisational systems, founder dependence, role ambiguity, and limited knowledge transfer"], a: 3, h: "Check exactly which rights are owned or licensed, for how long, in which territories, for what uses, and under whose approval." },
  { q: "A Nigerian film enterprise wants to evaluate whether its distinctive production culture can remain a competitive advantage as it expands into international co-productions. Which analysis most accurately combines organisational culture theory and the Resource-Based View?", o: ["Measure cultural strength only through employee satisfaction and ignore its effects on production capabilities and market outcomes", "Assess whether the culture is valuable, rare, difficult to imitate, transferable across contexts, and supported by formal systems", "Replace the local culture with standardised international procedures to ensure consistent competitive performance", "Assume that cultural distinctiveness automatically creates advantage regardless of scalability or organisational support"], a: 1, h: "Assess artistic and cultural purpose alongside access, evidence of impact, audience needs, and financial sustainability." },
  { q: "A streaming platform uses a freemium model: audiences receive limited content without payment, advertisers fund access, and premium subscribers pay for exclusive titles. Which media-economics analysis is most appropriate?", o: ["Examine cross-subsidisation, audience attention, price discrimination, network effects, advertiser demand, and conversion to paid tiers", "Classify the platform as a single-sided market because only subscribers generate economic value", "Focus solely on premium pricing to determine profitability, assuming that advertising revenue is independent of the total audience scale", "Treat free access as evidence that the content has no economic value to audiences or advertisers"], a: 0, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A Nigerian producer discovers that a proposed film’s strongest commercial opportunity lies in a franchise, but the initial project has uncertain demand and expensive development costs. How should real-options theory inform the investment decision?", o: ["Stage development and investment so early market evidence can inform expansion, continuation, licensing, or abandonment", "Value the franchise only by discounting the first film’s expected cash flows and excluding managerial flexibility", "Produce all planned sequels immediately because franchise value depends on simultaneous market entry", "Reject the project because uncertain demand eliminates the financial value of future expansion opportunities"], a: 0, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A film company wants to measure the quality of a documentary that has strong professional reviews, mixed audience responses, and significant civic impact among a small community. Which evaluation is conceptually strongest?", o: ["Integrate objective, subjective, and social dimensions rather than treating one stakeholder’s judgement as definitive", "Rely solely on professional reviews, as expert critics are the only reliable assessors of cinematic quality", "Use financial performance alone because quality is ultimately demonstrated by market demand", "Use audience ratings alone because social impact cannot be assessed as part of product quality"], a: 0, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A Nigerian media firm is deciding whether to adopt an emerging virtual-production technology. The technology may reduce location costs but requires staff retraining, new workflows, and uncertain compatibility with existing equipment. Which decision framework is most appropriate?", o: ["Evaluate strategic value, compatibility, complementarities, adoption costs, organisational readiness, learning effects, and competitive timing", "Base the decision solely on technical novelty and ignore organisational capabilities, market conditions, and implementation risks", "Adopt immediately because first movers always obtain sustainable advantage in media technology markets", "Reject the adoption because the high costs of staff retraining and new workflows suggest the technology is currently economically inefficient for the firm."], a: 0, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A Nigerian producer is assessing whether to release a politically sensitive feature through cinemas, a broadcaster, or an OTT platform. The project has high sunk costs, uncertain demand, possible regulatory scrutiny, and different revenue-sharing arrangements across outlets. Which analytical approach best captures the decision?", o: ["Select the outlet with the largest audience because reach determines total value", "Prioritize production costs and sunk costs as the primary metrics for selecting the most profitable distribution channel", "Combine political economy, industrial organisation, transaction costs, regulatory risk, windowing, and platform bargaining power", "Treat regulatory risk as irrelevant once the film has secured copyright protection"], a: 2, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A Nigerian film company has developed a distinctive production method that combines local cultural knowledge, trusted community relationships, and efficient low-budget workflows. Competitors can observe the final films but cannot easily reproduce the underlying relationships and tacit knowledge. Which theoretical explanation is strongest?", o: ["Sustainable advantage based on valuable, rare, difficult-to-imitate, knowledge-based resources", "Monopoly power established through the legal protection of intellectual property and copyright laws", "Economies of scale generated solely by owning professional equipment", "Market dominance caused by perfect information among competing producers"], a: 0, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A streaming platform commissions films using algorithmic forecasts, but the forecasts are based mainly on previous viewing behaviour. The resulting commissions increasingly resemble earlier successes, while experimental and minority-language projects receive less investment. Which critique is most theoretically defensible?", o: ["Recommendation data measures artistic quality more accurately than professional or social evaluation", "Algorithmic commissioning eliminates uncertainty because past behaviour perfectly predicts future demand", "Data-driven decision-making may create feedback loops, reinforce historical bias, and narrow innovation", "Reduced experimentation proves that audiences have no interest in culturally specific content"], a: 2, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A Nigerian producer and foreign partner disagree over whether the local producer’s cultural expertise should be treated as an equivalent contribution to the foreign partner’s cash investment. Which contractual and economic principle should guide the negotiation?", o: ["Divide control equally without valuing contributions or establishing procedures for unresolved disputes", "Value financial and non-financial contributions, then link control, recoupment, rights, and profit shares to agreed terms", "Allocate all control to the cash investor because only monetary contributions create economic value", "Transfer the local producer’s intellectual property permanently because foreign financing creates superior bargaining rights"], a: 1, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "A government seeks to support Nigeria’s film industry through tax incentives, training grants, local-content requirements, and anti-piracy enforcement. Policymakers must justify the intervention to both investors and citizens. Which evaluation is most complete?", o: ["Reject intervention because media markets should be governed exclusively by consumer demand and private ownership", "Assess market failure, cultural externalities, investment incentives, administrative capacity, political feasibility, and measurable industry outcomes", "Assume that state intervention enhances cultural diversity and boosts private-sector profits without considering potential drawbacks or market dynamics.", "Judge the policy solely by the number of films produced after implementation"], a: 1, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are producing a Nigerian historical drama when a distributor proposes replacing local political references with generic conflict so the film can travel more easily. How would you protect both market access and the film’s cultural value?", o: ["Preserve the story’s essential meanings while adapting context, subtitles, positioning, and promotional framing", "Remove all culturally specific references because international audiences cannot engage with local political meaning", "Let the distributor rewrite the film because international marketability determines its cultural value", "Reject every adaptation because any change to the original cut destroys cultural authenticity"], a: 0, h: "Check exactly which rights are owned or licensed, for how long, in which territories, for what uses, and under whose approval." },
  { q: "You are managing a multimedia start-up whose team includes filmmakers, software developers, marketers, and grant administrators. Each group uses different criteria to judge success, and decisions are becoming slow and adversarial. What should you do?", o: ["Give final authority to the department with the largest budget because financial control is the clearest measure of organisational importance", "Allow each department to pursue its own definition of success and reconcile disagreements only at project completion", "Standardise all evaluation around audience numbers because creative, technical, and social objectives cannot be compared meaningfully", "Establish shared objectives, decision rights, cross-functional processes, and criteria that recognise both creative and enterprise outcomes"], a: 3, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are offered an exclusive regional distribution deal for a film, but the distributor’s minimum-guarantee payment is tied to delivery and marketing conditions it has not clearly defined. What should you do before signing?", o: ["Transfer all regional rights immediately and negotiate technical and marketing conditions after the contract is executed", "Reject the deal because exclusive distribution agreements cannot accommodate performance conditions", "Clarify deliverables, acceptance standards, payment triggers, marketing duties, rights scope, audit access, and remedies for breach", "Accept the guarantee immediately, as a minimum payment makes any ambiguous delivery or marketing requirements financially insignificant."], a: 2, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are reviewing a failed multimedia campaign. It generated many impressions, but independent research shows that the intended audience misunderstood the message and did not change behaviour. What is the most defensible assessment?", o: ["Conclude that audience behaviour cannot be evaluated reliably in multimedia campaigns", "Distinguish exposure from comprehension and impact, then revise the message, targeting, and evaluation design", "Increase media spending without changing the message because misunderstanding results primarily from insufficient reach", "Treat the campaign as successful because impressions are the most objective indicator of communication effectiveness"], a: 1, h: "Distinguish audience size from meaningful engagement, reliable data, monetisation, and the platform’s bargaining power." },
  { q: "You are deciding whether to accept a government-backed production grant that requires local employment and cultural representation but may delay production through extensive reporting. How would you assess the offer?", o: ["Accept automatically because government support eliminates the need to evaluate contractual restrictions", "Reject the offer immediately, as public funding is inherently incompatible with independent creative decision-making.", "Focus only on the grant amount because reporting obligations have no effect on project value", "Compare funding value, compliance costs, creative autonomy, public benefits, schedule effects, and alternative financing"], a: 3, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are producing an interactive documentary when community participants disagree about how their testimonies should be edited and monetised. How would you protect both the project’s integrity and the participants’ interests?", o: ["Clarify consent, editorial boundaries, benefit expectations, withdrawal rights, and revenue arrangements before finalising the edit", "Assume that the initial release of testimony constitutes permanent consent for all future interactive and commercial applications", "Allow the highest-contributing funder to decide because financing determines ethical authority", "Remove participant involvement after recording to preserve complete editorial independence"], a: 0, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are preparing a film’s delivery package for several international platforms. Each requires different subtitles, aspect ratios, classifications, metadata, and technical specifications. What management approach best protects value?", o: ["Allow each platform to modify the film independently without producer oversight or version tracking to save time and resources.", "Delay technical planning until release because delivery requirements rarely affect distribution economics", "Build a rights-and-deliverables matrix linking territories, versions, approvals, costs, deadlines, and acceptance standards", "Produce one master file because digital platforms can adapt all technical and legal requirements automatically"], a: 2, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are offered a brand partnership for a film about environmental damage. The sponsor’s business practices are controversial, but its funding would substantially improve production quality. How should you evaluate the proposal?", o: ["Conceal the sponsorship to prevent audiences from questioning the film’s independence", "Assess reputational fit, audience trust, editorial safeguards, disclosure, funding dependence, and mission consequences", "Accept because increased production quality outweighs all concerns about sponsor credibility", "Reject the offer immediately, as any commercial sponsorship would inherently invalidate the film's social mission and integrity"], a: 1, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are managing a film project whose budget is being eroded by currency depreciation, imported equipment costs, and delayed investor payments. What is the most responsible response?", o: ["Maintain the original budget and schedule to avoid signaling managerial weakness or instability to your project investors", "Cut labour and safety costs first because they are the easiest expenses to reduce quickly", "Reforecast cash flow, prioritise essential costs, hedge or renegotiate exposure, preserve contingencies, and revise the schedule", "Borrow without a revised forecast because additional financing solves liquidity problems regardless of repayment risk"], a: 2, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are deciding whether to remake a successful foreign film for Nigerian audiences. The original has a proven commercial structure, but its humour, family relations, and social assumptions do not transfer directly. How would you create a viable adaptation?", o: ["Copy the original production design and marketing because cultural adaptation reduces brand recognition", "Remove all local cultural references to ensure the remake maintains an internationally universal appeal for a globalized audience", "Preserve the underlying dramatic function while redesigning characters, context, language, humour, rights, and audience positioning", "Translate the dialogue literally because commercial success depends mainly on retaining the original script"], a: 2, h: "Check exactly which rights are owned or licensed, for how long, in which territories, for what uses, and under whose approval." },
  { q: "You are preparing a co-production agreement when your foreign partner proposes that all disputes be resolved in its home country, using unfamiliar arbitration rules and a language not used in your production documents. What should you secure before accepting the deal?", o: ["Equal profit sharing, since it automatically resolves procedural imbalance", "A formal verbal assurance from the partner that any potential disputes are unlikely to ever arise", "Nigerian law only, without specifying a forum or enforcement process", "A balanced dispute clause covering forum, governing law, language, procedure, costs, and enforceability"], a: 3, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are producing a film with a child performer whose scenes may later be used in trailers, games, and promotional campaigns. What is the most responsible rights-management decision?", o: ["Obtain appropriate guardian consent and define each permitted use, duration, territory, medium, compensation, and safeguarding condition", "Use the original performance everywhere because consent for filming covers all future exploitation", "Avoid written terms because detailed releases may restrict creative flexibility", "Transfer all future rights to the distributor under the assumption that minors lack the legal capacity to hold or manage any relevant interests"], a: 0, h: "Check exactly which rights are owned or licensed, for how long, in which territories, for what uses, and under whose approval." },
  { q: "You are deciding whether to spend a limited marketing budget on a cinema premiere, targeted digital advertising, or influencer partnerships. Your audience research is incomplete, and each option produces different evidence about demand. How should you proceed?", o: ["Divide the budget equally because equal allocation produces neutral evidence", "Select the channel preferred by the director, as creative confidence is a reliable predictor of audience response.", "Spend the entire budget on the channel with the largest potential reach", "Use a staged, testable campaign with defined audience segments, conversion measures, and stop-or-scale criteria"], a: 3, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are reviewing an enterprise whose film earns revenue but repeatedly fails to pay crew on time, maintain insurance, or reserve funds for taxes and rights renewals. What deeper management problem should you identify?", o: ["A normal consequence of creative entrepreneurship that does not affect long-term enterprise value", "Weak governance and cash-flow controls that undermine operational, legal, and stakeholder sustainability", "A marketing problem that can be solved by increasing audience awareness", "An intellectual-property problem caused by insufficient copyright registration"], a: 1, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are asked to evaluate whether a film fund has improved Nigeria’s production ecosystem. The fund reports more films completed, but independent evidence shows little improvement in crew development, distribution access, revenue recovery, or production quality. What should your evaluation conclude?", o: ["The fund succeeded because increased production volume is the definitive measure of industry development", "Output growth alone is insufficient; assess additionality, quality, capacity, market access, sustainability, and unintended effects", "The fund’s impact is unmeasurable because cultural and economic outcomes are too distinct to be studied within a single evaluation framework.", "The fund failed because public support should produce immediate profitability for every recipient"], a: 1, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are reviewing a film whose social-media campaign produced intense debate, but the controversy came from misleading promotional claims that disappointed audiences after release. How would you evaluate the campaign’s effect on media value?", o: ["Measure success only through impressions and hashtag volume", "Conclude that negative reactions prove the film lacked artistic quality", "Treat controversy as successful engagement regardless of audience disappointment", "Distinguish attention from trust, satisfaction, retention, and long-term brand equity"], a: 3, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are negotiating with a platform that wants an exclusive first window but refuses to guarantee minimum promotion. Your film depends on visibility to create later television and licensing value. What should you seek?", o: ["Require unlimited promotion without specifying measurable obligations", "Accept exclusivity because platform availability guarantees audience discovery", "Reject windowing because all rights should be licensed simultaneously", "Define promotional commitments, window length, performance reporting, and rights reversion"], a: 3, h: "Check exactly which rights are owned or licensed, for how long, in which territories, for what uses, and under whose approval." },
  { q: "You are managing a production where a famous actor’s fee consumes funds needed for safety, sound, and post-production. Investors believe the actor will attract audiences, but no evidence confirms this for the film’s target market. How would you decide?", o: ["Replace the actor immediately, as high celebrity fees are inherently unjustifiable for a production's budget.", "Allocate the remaining budget to marketing because promotion compensates for underfunded production", "Retain the actor because star power always outweighs technical production standards", "Test the star’s incremental value against opportunity costs, audience fit, risk, and essential production quality"], a: 3, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are assessing a film proposal that promises international success but provides no evidence of rights clearance, audience research, distribution partners, or a realistic budget. What is the most serious weakness?", o: ["The proposal needs only a more famous director to become financially credible", "The proposal confuses aspiration with an investable plan and lacks evidence of feasibility", "Rights and distribution can be negotiated after financing is secured", "International ambition makes local audience research unnecessary"], a: 1, h: "Separate headline profitability from cash timing, risk, obligations, and the value of the resources being committed." },
  { q: "You are deciding whether to publish audience analytics from a documentary platform. The data may improve transparency but could expose small communities, reveal commercially sensitive behaviour, and encourage funders to reward only high-volume projects. What is the most responsible approach?", o: ["Withhold all analytics because audience data cannot ethically be used in media management", "Balance transparency with privacy, commercial confidentiality, representational fairness, and responsible interpretation", "Release only the most popular figures to present the project’s strongest performance clearly", "Publish all raw data immediately, as public access and total transparency should always outweigh confidentiality concerns"], a: 1, h: "Check exactly which rights are owned or licensed, for how long, in which territories, for what uses, and under whose approval." }
